> From Gennaro Contaldo on Food Tube / Jamie Oliver's channel. Adapted from his book *Let's Cook Italian*. Dough proofs at least 2 hours. For sauce, use simple garlic passata as in the video, or @./basics/Pizza Sauce{}.

== Dough ==

In a #bowl{}, mix @strong bread flour{500%g} with @salt{1%tsp} first (keep salt from sitting directly on the yeast). Add @dried yeast{7%g}(1 sachet) and @warm water{325%ml}. Mix with a spoon, then knead by hand — pull and fold until smooth.

Dust with a little flour, divide into 4 balls (about 150 g each). Dust a tray with @semolina{}(or breadcrumbs if you don't have semolina), set the dough balls on it, dust the tops with flour, cover with a damp cloth, and proof for at least ~{2%hours} until roughly doubled.

== Shape & Top ==

Press each ball from the center outward with your fingers, leaving a thicker rim (Neapolitan-style lip). Stretch gently from the edges — don't tear holes in the middle.

Spread a thin layer of @passata{1%cup}(or crushed tomatoes flavored with garlic; don't overload) from the center outward. Drizzle with @extra-virgin olive oil{}, and season with a little @salt{}.

Scatter @Parmesan cheese{} lightly over the tomato. Tear @mozzarella{200%g}(~50 g per pizza) by hand over the top. Tear @fresh basil{} over the pizza and drizzle with a little more olive oil.

== Bake ==

Dust a #pizza peel{} with semolina, transfer the pizza, and bake in a very hot oven (wood-fired if you have one; otherwise preheat your home oven as hot as it goes, ideally with a #pizza stone{}). Bake until the crust is blistered and the cheese is melted. Repeat for remaining pizzas.
